- Core issue: 4 raw files contained mixed metric types (percentage, mean, count, rate) collapsed into a single value column with no label preserved
  - Cleaning notebook filtered to percentage only before pivoting CI rows, silently discarding all absolute count and population rows
  - Result: clean files showed metric percentage for every row; population counts and scale-level data never made it through
  - Perceived health quality data appeared doubled; row counts were drastically reduced after cleaning
  - Scale context also lost: Stats Canada explicitly sets count scale to thousands, so a value like 17 means ~17 million people
- Fix agreed: Option B, split into 2 separate CSVs per dataset (percent file and count file), one source per metric type
  - Simpler than Option A since the pivot logic itself does not need to change
  - Affected datasets: stress coping, suicidal thoughts, perceived health, all-stats
  - Perceived health count values to be multiplied by 1,000 to restore the raw scale
  - Retain the raw Y-axis label column as a dedicated metric column in clean output
  - Pass a UOM column through every clean file so EDA can filter by measurement type without requiring deep knowledge of the source
- Backward compatibility: original file names kept as aliases so existing code does not break
